":["The stock trades 68% below its DCF fair value of $162, a meaningful margin of safety.","The fair-value models disagree (dispersion 58%), and the Monte Carlo range runs $115 to $239 (P10-P90), so treat the estimate as noisy.","On multiples it looks cheap against Consumer Cyclical peers: P/E 9.3 vs 19.3, EV/EBITDA 7.7 vs 11.2 and P/FCF 8.5 vs 16.5 (sector median).","Against its own history it is dear: the EBIT multiple sits 60% above its 10-year median.","Earnings yields are attractive: an FCF yield of 11.7% (above the risk-free rate) and an EBIT/EV of 11.8% (vs the 8% bar).","The 3 covering analysts target $51.67 on average, 2% below the price, well away from the model's fair value."],"prof":["ROIC of 118% clears the cost of capital of 7.0% by 111pp, the mark of a value creator; returns have swung a lot from year to year (CV 0.43).","It converts 19% of revenue into free cash flow.","Earnings quality looks solid: cash conversion of 1.14x.","It spends 0.8% of revenue on stock compensation."],"hlth":["Leverage is conservative at 0.5x net debt to EBITDA ($958.7M of cash against $1.5B of debt), with interest covered 11.6x."],"growth":["Revenue has compounded at 4.4% over 3 years, 2.9% over 5 years and 2.6% over 10 years.","At current returns, reinvesting 28% of profits supports about 30.0% a year of fundamental growth, enough to sustain growth.","Free cash flow has compounded 5.7% a year over five years.","Capex runs only 0.7x depreciation (2.1% of sales), so the asset base is being harvested rather than grown.","Its Rule of 40 score is 21, below the 40 that marks a healthy growth business.","It has beaten earnings estimates by 6.5% on average."],"own":["Insiders hold little stock (0.7%), and reported institutional holdings exceed 100% of shares (a sign of heavy share lending).","It returns 3.3% a year to shareholders (3.5% in dividends).","The share count has shrunk 6.8% a year over five years, steadily raising each holder's stake.","It has paid a dividend for 39 con